      <p>The headline pricing reads "$4.95 only" / "$4.99" on the VPS page, and the embedded plan-data confirms the promo monthly rates above. AccuWeb also publishes an "Earth + / Saturn + / Sun + / Galaxy +" variant set that trades RAM for higher transfer (e.g. VPS Earth + at 2 GB RAM / 60 GB SSD / 1.5 TB, VPS Galaxy + at 16 GB / 320 GB / 3 TB). <strong>Read the renewal rate:</strong> the standard non-promo monthly list is higher — VPS Edge $18.99, VPS Peak $37.99, VPS Power $75.99, VPS Supreme $151.99 — so the $4.95 entry is a promo you should lock annually if you want the low price to stick. The 4 GB RAM on the $4.95 Edge tier is notably generous for the price, though it ships with a single vCPU core.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Infrastructure — dedicated vCPUs, ECC RAM, RAID-10 SSD, SOC 2 Type II</h2>
      <p>The reason AccuWeb is a differentiator in this price band is its <strong>enterprise-grade infrastructure spec</strong>, verified from the official technical-specifications grid:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>Virtualization:</strong> KVM and Xen, with dedicated vCPUs and guaranteed CPU — no overselling.</li>
        <li><strong>Compute:</strong> Latest Intel Scalable and AMD EPYC processors.</li>
        <li><strong>Memory:</strong> ECC RAM (DDR4 / DDR5) for stability — a rarity at these price points.</li>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Enterprise-grade pure SSD, hardware RAID 6 &amp; RAID 10, plus a separate SSD backup array and off-site backups for disaster recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Network:</strong> 1 Gbps port, 1 dedicated IP included, DDoS mitigation up to 5 Gbps, customizable on-request firewall.</li>
        <li><strong>OS:</strong> Ubuntu, Debian, AlmaLinux, Rocky Linux (plus CloudLinux via control panel); full root access, SSH and VNC console access, easy OS reinstalls.</li>
        <li><strong>Management:</strong> Free and paid control panel options, real-time resource monitoring, easy CPU/RAM/storage scaling.</li>
        <li><strong>Reliability:</strong> 99.9% uptime SLA, SOC 2 Type II certified facilities, GDPR-ready infrastructure, under-15-minute response for critical issues, migration assistance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Network and datacenters — 16+ locations across six continents</h2>
      <p>AccuWeb deploys its VPS across <strong>16+ verified worldwide data center locations</strong>, which the official VPS page enumerates as: Denver, Hyderabad, New York, N. Virginia, Ohio, Oregon, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney, Tokyo, Montreal, Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris, Johannesburg, and Stockholm. The separate datacenter page confirms a broad global footprint spanning the US, Canada, Europe, APAC, India, and South Africa. This is a genuinely global reach that most budget hosts cannot match — ServerHub's 9 DCs are US + EU only, and RackNerd's footprint is US-concentrated. If you need a VPS physically close to users in Southeast Asia, India, or South Africa without going to a hyperscaler, AccuWeb is one of the few mid-budget options that covers those regions natively.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Pros and Cons</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-green-400 mb-3">Pros</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300">
            <li>✅ Dedicated vCPUs with guaranteed CPU — no overselling (KVM + Xen)</li>
            <li>✅ ECC RAM (DDR4 / DDR5) standard — rare in the budget VPS band</li>
            <li>✅ Enterprise pure SSD with hardware RAID 6 &amp; RAID 10, plus separate SSD backup array</li>
            <li>✅ 16+ global data centers (US, Canada, EU, APAC, India, Africa), SOC 2 Type II certified</li>
            <li>✅ 23-year track record (founded 2003), Vaghasia Group, 55,000+ accounts</li>
            <li>✅ Managed and unmanaged options with under-15-minute critical support</li>
            <li>✅ 99.9% uptime SLA, 1 Gbps port, dedicated IP, DDoS mitigation to 5 Gbps</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-400 mb-3">Cons</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300">
            <li>⚠️ Promo pricing renews at a higher standard list (Edge $18.99 → Supreme $151.99)</li>
            <li>⚠️ DDoS mitigation capped at 5 Gbps — below RackNerd/Vultr always-on in-line tiers</li>
            <li>⚠️ Entry VPS Edge ships only 1 vCPU — 4 GB RAM but single-core compute</li>
            <li>⚠️ Floor bandwidth is modest (250 GB on Edge) vs unlimited-bandwidth budget hosts</li>
            <li>⚠️ No public hourly billing — monthly plans without Vultr-style instant spin-up</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">How AccuWeb compares to RackNerd and Vultr</h2>
      <p>AccuWeb is best understood against the two anchors we cover most:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-gray-300 border border-gray-800 rounded-lg">
